Looking at this problem:

- We need the median of each sliding window of size `k`, with `n` up to 1e5, so we need roughly O(n log n).
- A robust approach: coordinate-compress the values, maintain a Fenwick tree (BIT) of counts for the current window, and find the median as the r-th smallest element using binary lifting on the Fenwick tree (O(log m) per query). Adding/removing window elements is O(log m) each.
- For odd `k`, the median is the ((k+1)/2)-th smallest; for even `k`, it's the average of the (k/2)-th and (k/2+1)-th smallest.

I verified this approach by hand on both examples (e.g., `nums = [1,3,-1,-3,5,3,6,7], k = 3` yields `[1,-1,-1,3,5,6]`, and the even-`k` averaging works, e.g. window `[5,2]` → `(2+5)/2 = 3.5`). The binary-lifting "kth element" finds the largest prefix index with count < r, whose 0-based position is exactly the r-th smallest value. Values fit in 64-bit ints, and sums of two middle values (~2^32) are exact in doubles, so precision is exact.

```php
class Solution {

    /**
     * @param Integer[] $nums
     * @param Integer $k
     * @return Float[]
     */
    function medianSlidingWindow($nums, $k) {
        $n = count($nums);

        // Coordinate compression of all values
        $vals = array_values(array_unique($nums));
        sort($vals);
        $m = count($vals);
        $pos = array_flip($vals);

        // Fenwick tree of counts (1-indexed)
        $tree = array_fill(0, $m + 1, 0);

        // Highest power of two <= m (for kth-element binary lifting)
        $log = 1;
        while (($log << 1) <= $m) {
            $log <<= 1;
        }

        $ans = [];

        for ($i = 0; $i < $n; $i++) {
            // Add incoming element nums[i]
            $j = $pos[$nums[$i]] + 1;
            for (; $j <= $m; $j += ($j & -$j)) {
                $tree[$j]++;
            }

            // Remove outgoing element nums[i-k]
            if ($i >= $k) {
                $j = $pos[$nums[$i - $k]] + 1;
                for (; $j <= $m; $j += ($j & -$j)) {
                    $tree[$j]--;
                }
            }

            // Emit median for window [i-k+1 .. i]
            if ($i >= $k - 1) {
                if ($k & 1) {
                    $ans[] = (float)$vals[$this->kthIndex($tree, $m, $log, ($k + 1) >> 1)];
                } else {
                    $half = $k >> 1;
                    $a = (float)$vals[$this->kthIndex($tree, $m, $log, $half)];
                    $b = (float)$vals[$this->kthIndex($tree, $m, $log, $half + 1)];
                    $ans[] = ($a + $b) / 2.0;
                }
            }
        }

        return $ans;
    }

    /**
     * 0-based index (into sorted unique values) of the r-th smallest
     * element (1-indexed r) in the multiset tracked by the Fenwick tree.
     */
    private function kthIndex($tree, $m, $log, $r) {
        $idx = 0;
        $rem = $r;
        for ($pw = $log; $pw > 0; $pw >>= 1) {
            $nxt = $idx + $pw;
            if ($nxt <= $m && $tree[$nxt] < $rem) {
                $idx = $nxt;
                $rem -= $tree[$nxt];
            }
        }
        return $idx;
    }
}
```

