Let me analyze this problem:

1. We have pairs of adjacent elements from an original array.
2. The endpoints of the original array appear exactly once in the pairs; all other elements appear exactly twice.
3. So we build an adjacency map, find an element that appears only once (an endpoint), then walk the chain.

Approach:
- Build a map from each value to its neighbors.
- Find a value that has only one neighbor (this is an endpoint).
- Start from that endpoint, walk to its neighbor, then repeatedly pick the neighbor that isn't the previous element.

In PHP, use arrays with integer keys (values can be negative, which is fine as PHP array keys can be negative integers).

Implementation:

```php
class Solution {
    /**
     * @param Integer[][] $adjacentPairs
     * @return Integer[]
     */
    function restoreArray($adjacentPairs) {
        $adj = [];
        foreach ($adjacentPairs as $pair) {
            $u = $pair[0];
            $v = $pair[1];
            $adj[$u][] = $v;
            $adj[$v][] = $u;
        }

        // Find endpoint (appears once)
        $start = 0;
        foreach ($adj as $key => $neighbors) {
            if (count($neighbors) == 1) {
                $start = $key;
                break;
            }
        }

        $n = count($adjacentPairs) + 1;
        $result = [$start];
        $prev = null;
        $cur = $start;

        while (count($result) < $n) {
            $neighbors = $adj[$cur];
            $next = ($neighbors[0] === $prev) ? $neighbors[1] : $neighbors[0];
            // careful: when cur is start, prev is null, neighbors[0] !== null so next = neighbors[0]
            $result[] = $next;
            $prev = $cur;
            $cur = $next;
        }

        return $result;
    }
}
```

