Looking at this problem:

- Values are bounded 1..50, so for each node we can check at most 50 candidate ancestor values.
- Do a DFS maintaining, for each value `v`, the deepest node with that value on the current root-to-node path (`last[v]`), encoded as `depth * n + node` so comparing encoded values finds the deepest one.
- Before entering a node, look up coprime values' `last` entries to find the closest coprime ancestor; then save/restore `last[v]` around the subtree traversal (save old value on the DFS stack frame, restore on backtrack).
- Use an iterative DFS to avoid recursion depth limits with n up to 1e5.

